## Results

### Global drivers (mean |SHAP| on P(default))

| Rank | Feature | mean \|SHAP\| |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `checking_status` | 0.111 |
| 2 | `duration_months` | 0.072 |
| 3 | `credit_amount` | 0.067 |
| 4 | `credit_history` | 0.056 |
| 5 | `savings_status` | 0.052 |
| 6 | `purpose` | 0.048 |
| 7 | `employment_since` | 0.032 |
| 8 | `other_installment_plans` | 0.031 |
| 9 | `personal_status_sex` | 0.027 |
| 10 | `installment_rate_pct` | 0.026 |

Matches the German Credit literature: the checking-account status dominates,
followed by loan size/duration and credit history. `personal_status_sex` sits at
#9 with a non-trivial 0.027 — flagged for the Phase 4 fairness audit.

### Worked local example — DENY (test-set max risk)

`P(default) = 0.968`, base `0.410`. Top contributions (toward DENY):
`checking_status = < 0 DM` (+0.121), `credit_history = no credits taken / all paid
back duly` (+0.098), `duration_months = 48` (+0.074), `property_type = unknown /
no property` (+0.061). SHAP and LIME share 4 of their top 5 features here.

> Note the counter-intuitive sign on `credit_history`: in this dataset "no credits
> taken" / "all paid back duly" is associated with *higher* risk than "critical
> account / other credits existing", because the latter group has a visible
> repayment track record. Worth calling out in the report — it is a data artefact,
> not a model bug.

### SHAP vs LIME agreement (40 test applicants, top-5)

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Sign agreement (shared features) | **0.97** |
| Jaccard overlap of top-5 sets | 0.43 ± 0.16 |
| Spearman rank correlation | 0.37 |

The two methods almost always agree on **which direction** a feature pushes the
decision; they agree less on exact rank and set membership, which is expected for
a sparse local linear surrogate vs exact Shapley values. Direction + magnitude of
the few largest contributions is all the Phase 4 reason-code generator needs, so
this level of agreement is acceptable. Written up in `reports/shap_vs_lime.md`.

